\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Humla (hut), also called Humla Tibetan, is a Tibetic language of Humla district, Nepal, with about 5,000 speakers. Speakers name it only generally or by village dialect. Searches found one sociolinguistic survey and background pages; no dictionary, grammar, or school materials were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Klaas de Vries (2014), \emph{A Sociolinguistic Survey of Humla Tibetan, Northwest Nepal}, Linguistic Survey of Nepal, Tribhuvan University (Glottolog record; contents beyond a wordlist and survey not confirmed). \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/552808}
\item Wikipedia: Humla Tibetan language. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humla_Tibetan_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Cultural Survival news on Nepal recognizing the Humlo people as an Indigenous nationality. Their language, Humlo Ke, is reported to differ greatly from Tibetan; whether it is the same as Humla Tibetan is unclear, so it should not be assumed identical. \url{https://www.culturalsurvival.org/news/nepal-officially-recognizes-humlo-peoples-indigenous-nationality}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Humla is not known to be taught in schools. Basic documentation (orthography, lexicon, grammar) would precede any curriculum work.

\end{document}
